When the node before the resource selection pointer is removed, Ladybird
now makes that node’s previous sibling the node before the pointer, at
any point in the algorithm. Gecko does the same, in HTMLMediaElement::
ContentWillBeRemoved(), which sets mSourcePointer to the removed child’s
previous sibling. Blink’s HTMLMediaElement::SourceWasRemoved() and
WebKit’s HTMLMediaElement::sourceWasRemoved() keep the next child to
consider instead, recomputing it from the current source node when that
child is removed. Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit
26.5, both new tests load the appended source in all three: the one that
swaps the sources while the element waits, and the one whose error
listener does it. They now do in Ladybird too.
